WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:42.000
This is 99% invisible. I'm Roman Mars. When new movies come out, most of the praise goes to the director and the lead actors, but there are so many other people involved in a film, and a lot of them are designers. They're a costume designers and set designers, but also graphic designers working behind the scenes on all of the graphic objects that you might see in a film. And I do mean every single graphic object. Anything with lettering on it, anything with pattern on it, and anything with illustration on it, and most things made out of paper. This is Annie Atkins. My name is Annie Atkins, and I'm a graphic designer for the film industry.
这里是“99%隐形”。我是罗曼·马尔斯。当新电影上映时，大部分的赞誉都归于导演和主演，但参与一部电影的还有许多其他人，其中很大一部分都是设计师。他们是服装设计师和布景设计师，同时也是平面设计师，在幕后负责你在电影中可能看到的各种平面物件。我是说每一个平面物件。任何有文字的、有图案的、有插画的东西，以及大部分用纸做成的东西。这是安妮·阿金斯。我叫安妮·阿金斯，我是电影行业的平面设计师。

2
00:00:42.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Over the course of her career, Atkins has designed all kinds of graphic props. Telegrams, vintage cigarette packaging, maps, love letters, books, poems, any kind of chocolate box or food packaging at all, labeling passports, fake CIA identification cards. And with all of these objects, Atkins needs to choose or design the lettering and make sure the paper is just right. All of these small design decisions
在其职业生涯中，阿金斯设计过各种各样的平面道具。电报、复古香烟包装、地图、情书、书籍、诗歌、各种巧克力盒或食品包装、护照标签、伪造的中央情报局身份证件。对于所有这些物件，阿金斯都需要选择或设计字体，并确保纸张恰到好处。所有这些微小的设计决定

3
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:33.000
that contribute to creating a cohesive, visual world for the film. As graphic designs film, we really have two main priorities when we start designing any proper set piece. The first is to set the period, and the second is to set the location. So that when the audience start watching the movie, they know immediately where the story has been told and when it's taking place.
都有助于为电影打造一个连贯的视觉世界。作为电影的平面设计师，当我们开始设计任何道具场景时，其实有两个主要优先级。第一个是确立时代，第二个是确立地点。这样当观众开始看电影时，他们能立即知道故事讲述的是哪里以及发生在哪一个时期。

4
00:01:33.000 --> 00:02:05.000
But I think the other thing that we have to do is we have to also try to tell the story at hand as well with graphics. So for example, if you see a sign in the background of a New York subway scene that says, walk, don't run, you can bet any seconds after you see that the characters are actually going to start running. All the graphic design you see in a film has a job to do. It's moving the story forward. Even if background signage seems to have a kind of a subliminal message,
但我认为我们还必须做的一件事是，也要尝试通过平面设计来讲述手头的故事。举个例子，如果你在纽约地铁场景的背景中看到一块标语写着“走，别跑”，你敢打赌，看到这个标语几秒钟后，角色们实际上就要开始跑了。你在电影中看到的每一项平面设计都有其使命。它在推动故事发展。即使背景标语似乎带有某种潜意识的信息，

5
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:35.000
it's always put their own purpose. Everything that we put in front of the camera is there for a reason. I met Annie Atkins at the AIGA National Design Conference, and I interviewed her on stage, and I just loved hearing her talk about her work. So I asked her to come on the show and talk more about it, and today we're just going to run part of that interview. So let's start at the beginning. How did you get into this business? My very first job was on a TV show called The Tudors, which we made here in Dublin and Ireland
它们也总是为了各自的目的而存在。我们放在镜头前的每一样东西都有其原因。我在AIGA国家设计大会上遇到了安妮·阿金斯，并在台上采访了她，我非常喜欢听她谈论自己的作品。所以我邀请她来到节目中多聊聊，今天我们只播放那次采访的一部分。那么，让我们从头开始。你是怎么进入这个行业的？我的第一份工作是在一部名为《都铎王朝》的电视剧中，大约十年前，我们在爱尔兰都柏林制作了这部剧。

6
00:02:35.000 --> 00:03:27.000
about 10 years ago now. And I went for an interview for the job as an assistant to the art department. But when they saw my portfolio, they realized I came from a graphic design background. They said, well, we're actually looking for a full-time graphic designer on the show to create all the graphic pieces. And I couldn't understand this because it didn't make sense why they would need a graphic designer on a show that was set in a period before graphic designers existed. I'd come from this advertising background where we were making logo design websites magazine layouts and there were none of these things in the court of Henry VIII, right? But then I learnt very quickly when I started the job that just because there weren't any graphic designers in the 15th century, it doesn mean there wasn't graphic designers, just that at that time it would have been the craftspeople who were the designers.
