they make the call whether they want to stick with historical accuracy or they just want to ignore that and design the newspaper to suit the story that they're telling. There's a line that gets bandied about in film art departments and it always goes something like we're not making a documentary about 19th century newspaper layout. We're telling a story. We're telling a story. Is there a part of you that really enjoys it when it's really accurate or do you get more pleasure from it just being really good and serving the story the best way possible or do you get the most excited when you can you know just kind of marry the two? It's exciting to study real references because what I find a lot of the time is that the truth
他们来决定是坚持历史真实性，还是忽略这一点，将报纸设计成契合他们正在讲述的故事的样式。在电影美术部门流传着这样一句话，通常类似于：我们不是在拍摄一部关于19世纪报纸排版的纪录片。我们是在讲故事。我们在讲故事。你心里有没有一部分真的很享受追求绝对真实，还是说你从它真的很棒并以最佳方式服务于故事中获得更多快乐，或者当你能够，你知道，将两者完美结合时，你会感到最兴奋？研究真实的参考资料令人兴奋，因为我发现很多时候真相

22
00:12:28.000 --> 00:13:00.000
is more interesting. If I started a prop with just a blank page and illustrator, I wouldn't be able to create something as interesting as what has actually been designed before me. My imagination can't compare to the collective imagination of the hundreds and thousands of craft people that have gone before me over the last hundreds of years. So it's interesting to look at something real and then develop it with the director and the production designer to suit the story
比想象更有趣。如果我只从Illustrator里的一张空白页开始做一个道具，我是无法创作出像摆在我面前的那些历经岁月设计出来的东西那样有趣的内容的。我的想象力无法与过去几百年来无数工匠的集体想象力相提并论。所以，观察真实的事物，然后与导演和美术指导一起根据故事进行开发，是一件很有趣的事情，

